WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:05.580
IGNATIUS: Ladies and gentlemen, if you could
finish eating this delicious lunch that the

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:06.840
Council has provided.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.559
I'm David Ignatius, a columnist for The Washington
Post.

00:00:09.559 --> 00:00:15.830
It's my pleasure to introduce Senator Chris
Coons, who's going to offer an assessment

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:19.160
of the Iran deal one year later.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:25.330
It's amazing to me, when I think back a year
ago we talked of almost nothing other than

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:32.860
the Iran nuclear, this is a rare moment when
we all get together and think about how it's

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:34.060
going.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:40.969
Senator Coons, as I think everyone here understands,
junior senator from Delaware, but someone

00:00:40.969 --> 00:00:49.430
who paid special attention to the issues in
this negotiation, followed very closely, was

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:50.430
skeptical.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:56.000
So when he announced his decision to support
the agreement, that really was the decisive

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.000
moment.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:05.089
He and Senator Casey together brought the
vote total to what the president needed.

00:01:05.089 --> 00:01:10.860
I'm just going to read a quote from a Washington
Post story the day we were reporting, September

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.030
1 last year, Senator Coons decision.

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:19.350
"Disclosed in an exclusive interview with
The Washington Post, delivered a powerful

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:24.939
blow to opponents of the plan because the
Delaware Democrat had previously voiced some

00:01:24.939 --> 00:01:28.210
of the deepest skepticism about the controversial
deal."

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.240
So I really do think that was a key moment
a year ago.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:37.680
I'm especially interested to hear how Senator
Coons evaluates things a year later.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.320
So, Senator.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.140
(Applause.)

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:47.800
COONS: Well, thank you, David.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:51.880
Thank you both for the kind introduction and
for your willingness to moderate today's conversation.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:57.180
And I'd like to also thank the Council on
Foreign Relations for hosting this discussion

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.640
of the Iran nuclear agreement one year on.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.960
Today, as we approach the one-year anniversary
of the announcement of the Joint Comprehensive

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:12.300
Plan of Action, or the JCPOA, I will evaluation
whether this deal has succeeded so far, and

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:17.860
explain what I've done to honor my commitment
to aggressively oversee its implementation

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:19.980
and its enforcement.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.700
Last year, as David just mentioned, after
closely scrutinizing the terms of the deal,

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:30.110
I concluded that it represented the least-bad
option for achieving one of America's most

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:37.220
important-(audio break)-from Iran from developing
or obtaining a nuclear weapon.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:41.970
Since implementation day of this agreement
in January, we have seen real and meaningful

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.150
progress toward that goal.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:49.310
As I suspect everyone in this room knows,
but is important to repeat, Iran has shipped

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:54.650
12 tons of enriched uranium-virtually its
entire stockpile-out of the country.

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:59.060
The Iranian regime has reduced by two thirds
the number of its functioning uranium enrichment

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:00.090
centrifuges.

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:05.950
And it has permanently reengineered the Arak
Heavy Water Reactor by filling its core with

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:06.950
concrete.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:12.700
These three steps in combination cut off Iran's
most likely short-term pathways to a nuclear

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.730
weapon with either uranium or plutonium.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:20.720
Iran has also give the International Atomic
Energy Agency, or the IAEA, the world's nuclear

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:27.780
watchdog, unprecedented 24/7 access to monitor
all of its declared nuclear facilities.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:33.860
That access covers Iran's entire nuclear supply
chain, from Iranian mines and mills to centrifuge

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:39.370
production and enrichment facilities-every
known nuclear site in Iran.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:44.230
These steps have, for now, frozen Iran's ability
to develop a nuclear weapon.

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:48.430
And as a result of Iran's initial compliance
with the agreement, the time for it to break

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:53.780
out, to assemble enough fissile material for
one nuclear weapon has extended significantly

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:56.690
from just two or three months to now more
than a year.

00:03:56.690 --> 00:04:01.540
So measured by whether the agreement has prevented
Iran from developing or obtaining a nuclear

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.400
weapon, this deal has, so far, been successful.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:10.430
I also, though, have long suspected Iran that
would seek to push the boundaries of the deal

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:16.100
in ways both minor or major, to test how the
United States and our allies would respond.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:21.019
Earlier this week, I had an in-person meeting
with Ambassador Steve Mull, the Obama administration's

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:23.940
lead coordinator for Iran nuclear implementation.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:29.340
And I heard what I hoped for, and what I expected,
that the Departments of State, Treasury, Energy,

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:34.520
and our intelligence community are working
closely together to aggressively monitor and

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:37.040
enforce the JCPOA.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:41.930
American officials also are communicating
regularly and closely with their Iranian counterparts,

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:45.680
with the European Union, and the other key
parties to the deal.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.050
And as I anticipated, there have been a handful
of occasions in the past year in which the

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:55.430
United States has had to mobilize our P5+1
partners to hold Iran closely to the parameters

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:57.570
of the JCPOA.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:59.250
Here's a public example.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:05.510
When Iran's heavy water stockpile in February
briefly reached 130.9 metric tons, exceeding

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:12.840
the JCPOA limit of 130 metric tons, the IAEA
quickly observed this anomaly, reported it

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:16.140
to the partner countries, we took action,
and Iran addressed it.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:21.130
There are several other incidents I can't
describe in public, but in each case the United

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:27.540
States rallied our international partners
to promptly address a discrepancy or disagreement

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:30.970
and successfully enforce this direct interpretation
of the deal.

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:37.120
In the years to come-in the many years to
come-we have to focus on continuing aggressive

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:41.680
enforcement, and congressional oversight will
remain critical.

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:46.220
But we also have to continue to push back
on Iran's destabilizing and provocative actions

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:50.760
outside the four corners, the parameters of
the nuclear agreement.

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:54.480
From its repeated calls for the destruction
of Israel, to its support for terrorism in

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:59.870
Syria and Iraq and Yemen, to its ongoing illegal
ballistic missile tests, and its human rights

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:06.530
violations, Iran is not a responsible state
seeking to rejoin the international community.

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:11.820
These actions underscore an important point
about the deal: The JCPOA was, is, and will

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:16.790
mostly likely remain a transactional, not
a transformational, agreement.

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:21.690
This deal seeks to prevent Iran from developing
a nuclear bomb, not to bring Iran into the

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.240
community of nations.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:27.810
Only a genuine change in direction by the
Iranian regime can do that.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:33.220
Rather, though, than walking away from the
deal, in my view the United States must remain

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:37.340
engaged, and to lead the international community's
enforcement of the agreement.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:42.920
As I tell my Senate colleagues regularly,
whether you opposed or supported the JCPOA

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:47.270
last year, we have a shared interest in working
together this year and in the future to make

00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:49.990
sure this deal succeeds.

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:54.810
With that said, let me briefly review broadly
where we are today, nearly a year into the

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:56.060
deal.

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:58.340
Two things really haven't changed at all.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.520
First, Iran remains untrustworthy.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:06.639
Since its 1979 revolution, Iran has pursued
interests and advocated values that are completely

00:07:06.639 --> 00:07:08.590
opposed to those of the West.

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:11.990
And its approach to regional crises has not
changed.

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:16.200
Second, Iran continues to exploit weak states
and power vacuums.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:20.770
And we need to disrupt Iran's destabilizing
activities in the Middle East and to support

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:25.690
our regional partners, most importantly by
promptly concluding a strengthened 10-year

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:30.010
memorandum of understanding on defense priorities
with Israel.

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:32.260
But two additional points I think are worth
emphasizing.

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:37.710
Broadly, the last year has shown that international
agreement and multilateral diplomacy can be

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:41.240
effective, even with rogue states like Iran.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:43.950
There is value in talking with our enemies.

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:50.139
Second, we're today engaged in a public disagreement
with Iran about whether it is, in fact, seeing

00:07:50.139 --> 00:07:52.430
the benefits of the deal.

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:56.260
When Valiollah Seif, the governor of Iran's
Central Bank, spoke here at the Council on

00:07:56.260 --> 00:08:01.440
Foreign Relations earlier this year in April,
he argued that the United States and our European

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:08.020
allies had not honored our JCPOA obligations,
because Iran hasn't been able to access the

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:12.430
global financial system, and hasn't realized
the economic benefits they foresaw from this

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:13.430
deal.

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:14.430
But here's how I see it.

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:17.639
The United States has upheld our end of the
bargain.

00:08:17.639 --> 00:08:21.980
We have not prevented Iran from receiving
economic benefits associated with the deal.

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:26.900
In fact, their recent activity, significant
increases in oil exports, billions of dollars

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:32.339
in recent deals, and projected economic growth
of 3 to 5 percent casts real doubt on Iran's

00:08:32.339 --> 00:08:33.339
claims.

00:08:33.339 --> 00:08:38.700
In my view, Iran alone is responsible for
making its market an attractive and safe place

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.339
in which to do business.

00:08:40.339 --> 00:08:45.320
And for many corporations and individuals,
Iran is neither attractive, nor safe.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:50.650
And I am concerned that by entertaining Iranian
complaints of inadequate sanctions relief

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:53.510
we risk giving these claims legitimacy.

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:58.501
If Iran is unhappy with the level of economic
benefits its received since the JCPOA, it

00:08:58.501 --> 00:09:01.200
has only itself and its own actions to blame.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:07.010
Today, as we look beyond year one of the JCPOA
and look ahead to five, 10, or 15 years from

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:13.170
now, one thing is clear: If this agreement
is to succeed long term, engaged congressional

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:16.060
oversight remains essential.

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:20.820
This oversight falls into two categories,
starting with congressional pushback on Iran's

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:23.820
bad behavior outside the deal's parameters.

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:27.470
That's why I've continued to be outspoken
on the floor of the Senate in calling for

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:32.350
stronger efforts to interdict Iranian arms
shipments to the Houthi rebels in Yemen, and

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:35.470
in celebrating those interdictions that have
occurred.

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:40.510
I've also asked foreign leaders, including
those from Israel, Saudi Arabia, Qatar, India,

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:45.380
and Russian, about how to strengthen our efforts
together to counter Iranian aggression.

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:51.410
And I've urged Congress to provide this administration
with unilateral authority to address Iran's

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:56.150
ongoing ballistic missile tests, given the
inertia in the U.N. Security Council.

00:09:56.150 --> 00:10:00.560
I've called on the administration as well
to levy additional sanctions against IRGC-affiliated

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:05.210
entities, like Mahan Aar, which Treasury has
done.

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:09.550
And I've worked to secure increased funding
for Treasury's Office of Terrorism and Financial

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:12.350
Intelligence, which enforces these sanctions.

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:16.399
But Congress also has to remain engaged in
overseeing the enforcement of the nuclear

00:10:16.399 --> 00:10:17.630
deal itself.

00:10:17.630 --> 00:10:23.790
In January, I joined a congressional delegation
in visiting IAEA headquarters in Vienna, where

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:26.280
we met with Director General Amano.

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:31.470
The JCPOA allows hundreds of nuclear inspectors
to oversee Iran's nuclear program through

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:36.890
both remote online enrichment monitoring and
intrusive in-person inspections.

00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:41.860
These inspectors, trained in America's unique
national labs, are funded by the United States

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:47.730
Congress, and utilize cutting-edge technology,
largely developed by our scientists.

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:53.510
Because we are asking the IAEA to engage in
an unprecedented level of oversight, Director

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:58.660
General Amano has recently publicly raised
concerns that his agency is stretched thin

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:00.930
as it works to implement this deal.

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:07.930
That's why Congress must do our part to ensure
the IAEA has reliable, long-term resources.

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:12.960
This IAEA responsibility has taken on an even
greater importance this week, given recent

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:17.970
news that that agency has detected signs of
North Korean plutonium reprocessing.

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:24.960
The IAEA responsibilities remain global and
important, and need to be effectively sustained.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:29.890
I've also repeatedly called on Congress to
take full advantage of the IAEA's unprecedented

00:11:29.890 --> 00:11:35.279
access to Iran's nuclear program, by increasing
our voluntary contribution to the agency,

00:11:35.279 --> 00:11:38.270
which would encourage our international partners
to do so as well.

00:11:38.270 --> 00:11:42.790
In addition, I've pressed for the confirmation
of Laura Holgate as U.S. ambassador to the

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:45.340
U.N. offices in Vienna, which includes the
IAEA.

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:50.410
And I've called for the renewal of the Iran
Sanctions Act, so that we have a viable mechanism

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:55.180
to snap back sanctions in Iran violations
the JCPOA.

00:11:55.180 --> 00:12:00.430
I intend to uphold my commitment to oversee
strict enforcement of this deal, regardless

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:02.970
of who is elected president this fall.

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:07.620
As our moderator this afternoon recently wrote
in a column: The Iran nuclear agreement deserves

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:10.360
far more attention in this presidential campaign.

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:12.839
And I couldn't agree more.

00:12:12.839 --> 00:12:18.279
If we abandon the JCPOA, we don't end up with
a better agreement, nor do we end up with

00:12:18.279 --> 00:12:19.440
no agreement.

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:23.850
Instead, our partners around the world will
most likely develop a new strategy to deal

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:27.860
with Iran without American leadership or input.

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:31.840
If we abandon this agreement, we signal that
we're giving up on diplomacy.

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:33.870
We could lose valuable intelligence.

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:36.230
We would forego the support of our allies.

00:12:36.230 --> 00:12:41.500
And we would close vital communications channels
to senior Iranian leaders that have helped

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:44.140
to restrain Iran's nuclear program.

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:49.570
Frankly, I think that's exactly what the most
conservative elements in Iran's regime want.

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:52.020
But let me be clear about that.

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:57.040
We can, and we should, distinguish between
the Iranian regime and the Iranian people.

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:01.530
The people of Iran continue to turn out at
the polls to vote, even in elections that

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:03.910
are not truly free nor fair.

00:13:03.910 --> 00:13:07.530
They have repeatedly demonstrated in the streets
for democracy and engagement, risking their

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:09.090
lives to do so.

00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:14.290
The Iranian people deserve our support in
their struggle for human rights and democracy,

00:13:14.290 --> 00:13:19.300
especially during this sacred month of Ramadan,
as Muslims around the world reflect on their

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:20.980
hopes for peace.

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:26.290
But the Iranian regime deserves condemnation
for a decades-long pattern of human rights

00:13:26.290 --> 00:13:29.750
abuses, support for terrorism, and other bad
behavior.

00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:35.839
The government of Iran, the Iranian regime,
remains a dangerous revolutionary government,

00:13:35.839 --> 00:13:41.690
and it will continue to present a potential
nuclear threat for decades to come.

00:13:41.690 --> 00:13:47.089
Preventing Iran from obtaining a nuclear weapon
will require steady leadership, an understanding

00:13:47.089 --> 00:13:52.860
of the complexities of international diplomacy,
and constant scrutiny of Iran's behavior.

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:57.350
These tasks must be the responsibility not
just of this president this year, but a top

00:13:57.350 --> 00:14:02.410
priority for the next president, the intelligence
community, and members of Congress for many

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:03.830
years to come.

00:14:03.830 --> 00:14:08.600
That's why I intend to stay actively engaged
in monitoring enforcement of the deal, in

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:12.970
advocating for a strong foreign policy that
supports our allies in the Middle East and

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:16.300
promotes American interests and values around
the world.

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:18.850
Thank you.

00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:20.120
(Applause.)

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:27.760
IGNATIUS: Senator, thank you for that.

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:34.480
It was excellent and raised all of the basic
issues.

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:38.670
I just want to dig on some of them.

00:14:38.670 --> 00:14:44.300
You can't, in our business, let a news cycle
pass without talking about Donald Trump, as

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:45.300
we all know.

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:46.300
(Laughter.)

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:51.230
So I'm going to begin by just asking you about
what the presumptive Republican nominee has

00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:55.130
said about the Iran nuclear deal.

00:14:55.130 --> 00:15:01.830
The most frequent comment has been it's a
bad deal and I would have negotiated a better

00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:02.830
deal.

00:15:02.830 --> 00:15:08.720
He implied in some comments that he would
seek to reopen the negotiation and get that

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:12.830
better deal that he thinks was possible.

00:15:12.830 --> 00:15:17.680
In other comments he's implied, but I don't
want to push this too hard but certainly I

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:20.300
came away with the sense, that he might repudiate
the deal.

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:23.500
It was badly negotiated in his judgement.

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:28.700
So I want to ask you directly, what would
be the consequence of either seeking to reopen

00:15:28.700 --> 00:15:36.500
the negotiations if the next president concluded
it was a bad deal, or in some way repudiating

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:37.500
the deal?

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:38.570
What would happen if that were done?

00:15:38.570 --> 00:15:44.420
COONS: Well, I think without the strong and
united support of our partners in the deal,

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:49.250
seeking to reopen it and renegotiate it is
a fool's errand, and would simply lead to

00:15:49.250 --> 00:15:54.890
a mess, to a hash, to an apprehension on the
part of our key allies that we were withdrawing.

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:57.660
It would lead to more aggressive actions by
the Iranians.

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:03.390
I suspect it would lead them to take stronger
steps that indicated a willingness to return

00:16:03.390 --> 00:16:07.420
to more active nuclear enrichment and more
active nuclear research.

00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:13.430
Or, it might very well lead to a greater distance
between the United States and our core European

00:16:13.430 --> 00:16:14.430
allies.

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:21.329
It depends on what else the Donald sought
to renegotiate, given his alarming and irresponsible

00:16:21.329 --> 00:16:28.920
statements about NATO, about the nuclear umbrella
for South Korea and Japan, about a whole range

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:31.370
of other topics in foreign policy.

00:16:31.370 --> 00:16:36.120
My concern is that it wouldn't be just this
issue on the table, and if all those different

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:38.520
things were afoot at the same time.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:45.269
One of the core principles of American leadership
in foreign policy, which is to be predictable-particularly

00:16:45.269 --> 00:16:50.089
in your commitment to certain alliances and
certain treaties and certain obligations-would

00:16:50.089 --> 00:16:51.120
be overthrown.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:56.209
And a lot of our vital allies would be puzzled
and concerned, and particularly with regard

00:16:56.209 --> 00:17:04.020
to Iran, I think likely to renegotiate the
deal around us for their economic and security

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:07.509
benefit, and to rely far lesson American involvement.

00:17:07.509 --> 00:17:12.299
As I tried to reference in my remarks, we
are absolutely essential to the successful

00:17:12.299 --> 00:17:14.399
implementation of this deal.

00:17:14.399 --> 00:17:19.539
America's national labs, America's contributions
to the IAEA, the engagement of our intelligence

00:17:19.539 --> 00:17:25.220
community, our role in leading the Atlantic
community I think are all essential to this

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:26.819
deal being successfully implemented.

00:17:26.819 --> 00:17:33.359
I think an effort at renegotiating it, even
a bluster, half-hearted attempt, would be

00:17:33.359 --> 00:17:34.359
unconstructive.

00:17:34.359 --> 00:17:36.940
IGNATIUS: That's very direct and helpful.

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:44.570
I want to pursue one intriguing thing you
said in your opening comments, where you made

00:17:44.570 --> 00:17:49.169
reference to certain discrepancies that have
arisen.

00:17:49.169 --> 00:17:53.539
You said you couldn't discuss the details
of several of them.

00:17:53.539 --> 00:17:59.869
You know, we hear rumors about this, but obviously
we don't have access to the information that

00:17:59.869 --> 00:18:00.950
you do.

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:07.269
I want to ask you, not for the details that
you can't share, but for your analytical judgement

00:18:07.269 --> 00:18:09.749
about what they tell us.

00:18:09.749 --> 00:18:14.940
In these discrepancies, do the Iranians seem
to be pushing at the limits?

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:18.799
Are there genuine differences in reading the
language?

00:18:18.799 --> 00:18:21.259
Are they accidental?

00:18:21.259 --> 00:18:26.460
What's going on in the cases that have arisen
that you have been able to look at?

00:18:26.460 --> 00:18:33.369
COONS: Well, a good friend of mine, who had
long business dealings with Iranians in the

00:18:33.369 --> 00:18:39.480
'40s, '50s, and '60s, told me a story before
the deal entered into effect.

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:45.230
And it's a long story, but the punchline was,
after several years of painful, detailed negotiations

00:18:45.230 --> 00:18:49.679
at the signing day where they entered into
a new business arrangement, his new Iranian

00:18:49.679 --> 00:18:54.190
partner said: All right, now the negotiations
begin.

00:18:54.190 --> 00:18:59.450
I think they have a long demonstrated history
of actively and aggressively litigating the

00:18:59.450 --> 00:19:02.399
boundaries of almost every arrangement they've
been involved in.

00:19:02.399 --> 00:19:04.690
Part of that is cultural.

00:19:04.690 --> 00:19:09.610
Part of that is the real gulf between our
values and priorities and our strategic interest

00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:11.389
and theirs.

00:19:11.389 --> 00:19:17.249
I think there is an open question whether
the excursions and challenges there have been

00:19:17.249 --> 00:19:24.159
are the result of internal miscommunication,
whether they are intentional testing of the

00:19:24.159 --> 00:19:25.739
West's resolve.

00:19:25.739 --> 00:19:28.779
But I think we are safest presuming the latter.

00:19:28.779 --> 00:19:34.960
And I think we are safest demonstrating a
robust and sustained and close engagement,

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:40.489
making sure that we are not over-litigating
in response, but promptly and appropriately

00:19:40.489 --> 00:19:45.039
and thoroughly responding right up until the
moment we get an action.

00:19:45.039 --> 00:19:49.059
One of the lessons we've learned this year
is that the Iranians respond to pressure.

00:19:49.059 --> 00:19:52.859
I think that was one of the lessons of the
international sanctions campaign that brought

00:19:52.859 --> 00:19:53.899
them to the table.

00:19:53.899 --> 00:20:00.190
I think that's one of the lessons of repeated
efforts to identify and call out specific

00:20:00.190 --> 00:20:04.899
moments when they are outside the well-known
boundaries of the JCPOA, even by a tiny amount,

00:20:04.899 --> 00:20:06.489
even by a footfall.

00:20:06.489 --> 00:20:13.289
And I think we see a division within the Iranian
government and regime as to what their long-term

00:20:13.289 --> 00:20:18.539
prospects really are, whether they're going
to get significant economic benefit or whether

00:20:18.539 --> 00:20:24.440
they should, frankly, give up on that and
turn inward and turn more to active-return

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:28.470
to more active confrontation with the West,
in part through a nuclear program.

00:20:28.470 --> 00:20:36.130
IGNATIUS: Let me ask you to focus on that
last issue, of what benefits Iran gets as

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:38.549
a result of the agreement.

00:20:38.549 --> 00:20:49.470
It's clear from comments that there is a genuine
sense that the benefits we expected, the opening

00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:55.710
in terms of financial markets, opportunities,
just haven't arrived.

00:20:55.710 --> 00:21:02.330
You gave a good account of why that's not
our fault in our opening comments, but even

00:21:02.330 --> 00:21:10.220
so in terms of the durability of this agreement,
an agreement that people-officials and the

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:17.950
public in the effected country-don't feel
benefits them end up weaken the agreement.

00:21:17.950 --> 00:21:26.450
Secretary Lew at Treasury noted that sanctions
lose their force if the removal of sanctions

00:21:26.450 --> 00:21:27.450
doesn't bring benefits.

00:21:27.450 --> 00:21:31.320
I thought it was a powerful comment that he
made a few months ago.

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:34.220
So if you could address this.

00:21:34.220 --> 00:21:40.639
Not to argue the case for Iran, but isn't
it a fact that if they don't get benefits

00:21:40.639 --> 00:21:48.320
from having made this deal, support for it
and its integrity, its vitality will diminish?

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:53.409
COONS: Well, first, they are getting real
and concrete economic benefits out of this

00:21:53.409 --> 00:21:54.409
deal.

00:21:54.409 --> 00:21:59.249
They are shipping and selling their oil on
world markets at a level unseen in recent

00:21:59.249 --> 00:22:00.249
years.

00:22:00.249 --> 00:22:04.072
And they will eventually get all the economic
benefit that comes from being able to sell

00:22:04.072 --> 00:22:06.390
their oil on global markets.

00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:12.190
I'll remind you, the price of a barrel of
oil has been cut more than half since they

00:22:12.190 --> 00:22:15.960
entered into the negotiations about this deal.

00:22:15.960 --> 00:22:21.149
And that's just one way in which their expectations
about how much benefit they would receive

00:22:21.149 --> 00:22:24.630
and how quickly have fallen far short of their
expectations.

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:27.190
So their economy is growing again.

00:22:27.190 --> 00:22:30.229
They are receiving access to global oil markets.

00:22:30.229 --> 00:22:33.779
They are signing lots of big agreements with
international firms.

00:22:33.779 --> 00:22:38.759
But they are also, as I pointed out in detail,
continuing to take actions that would make

00:22:38.759 --> 00:22:44.600
any rational corporation, individual, and
global banking entity question whether this

00:22:44.600 --> 00:22:47.529
was a good economy in which to invest.

00:22:47.529 --> 00:22:51.090
Lots of economic players in the region moved
on during the years when sanctions really

00:22:51.090 --> 00:22:53.239
cut off the Iranian market.

00:22:53.239 --> 00:22:56.629
And here are plenty of other places of opportunity
that are less risky.

00:22:56.629 --> 00:22:59.210
And their economy remains opaque.

00:22:59.210 --> 00:23:04.139
The IRGC, the Revolutionary Guard Corps, has
a significant role throughout the economy.

00:23:04.139 --> 00:23:07.049
And it's important to remember the Iran is
not monolithic.

00:23:07.049 --> 00:23:11.730
The IRGC has been one of the biggest beneficiaries
of sanctions.

00:23:11.730 --> 00:23:17.059
And so for sanctions and their impact, the
sort of stasis it's imposed on their economy

00:23:17.059 --> 00:23:21.389
to unwind, a lot of the economic position
of the IRGC is at risk.

00:23:21.389 --> 00:23:27.059
So in order for them to completely open up
their market and be transparent so that Western

00:23:27.059 --> 00:23:31.889
entities can assess the risk they're taking
in doing transactions, the IRGC's role has

00:23:31.889 --> 00:23:33.019
to change.

00:23:33.019 --> 00:23:36.929
It is a corrupt, opaque, militarily controlled
economy.

00:23:36.929 --> 00:23:40.859
And if I were sitting in a board room trying
to predict which country we ought to next

00:23:40.859 --> 00:23:43.869
try and move into, this wouldn't be on my
top ten.

00:23:43.869 --> 00:23:49.090
But that's not a result of American intransigence
or a refusal by the West to grant them access

00:23:49.090 --> 00:23:52.020
to the benefits of the deal.

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:58.419
IGNATIUS: President Obama, certainly the last
year of the Iran nuclear debate, I think tried

00:23:58.419 --> 00:24:03.940
to lowball expectations about the transforming
power.

00:24:03.940 --> 00:24:08.619
As you said in your remarks, it was transactional
not transformative.

00:24:08.619 --> 00:24:14.739
Even so, I think anybody who looks at Iran
has an idea that this might be a process of

00:24:14.739 --> 00:24:22.229
gradual political change, in which more moderate
forces- a Zarif, Rouhani who supported the

00:24:22.229 --> 00:24:24.360
deal, were empowered.

00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:27.470
Over time, you had movement to a different
leadership.

00:24:27.470 --> 00:24:34.549
Some people see the elections for the Iranian
parliament as reinforcing that idea.

00:24:34.549 --> 00:24:40.859
So I want to ask you, without-I'm not asking
for the starry-eyed, gee-whiz, but what do

00:24:40.859 --> 00:24:48.260
you think of the prospects for gradual evolution
of Iran as it rejoins the global economy?

00:24:48.260 --> 00:24:54.460
Are we going to see a different country, in
your judgement, over the next five to 10 years?

00:24:54.460 --> 00:24:57.200
COONS: Over the next five years, I wouldn't
expect that.

00:24:57.200 --> 00:25:00.749
Over the next 10 to 20, I think it is possible.

00:25:00.749 --> 00:25:07.840
Part of what I tried to convey was a respect
for the depth, complexity, sophistication

00:25:07.840 --> 00:25:10.179
of the Persian people, of the culture of Iran.

00:25:10.179 --> 00:25:15.529
They are an incredibly educated, sophisticated
people, with a lot of resources and a lot

00:25:15.529 --> 00:25:17.350
of capabilities.

00:25:17.350 --> 00:25:24.359
But the orientation, the ideology of the current
regime in any-in any reasonable prediction

00:25:24.359 --> 00:25:28.349
of how they're likely to behave in the next
couple of years, given the region, given their

00:25:28.349 --> 00:25:35.019
current engagements, given their actions in
Iraq, and Syria, Yemen, and elsewhere, I think

00:25:35.019 --> 00:25:39.599
only suggests a hardening of the position
of the regime in the short term.

00:25:39.599 --> 00:25:45.029
Given the election in the Majlis, given some
of the demographic dynamics, given the underlying

00:25:45.029 --> 00:25:47.859
culture, I think it's entirely possible.

00:25:47.859 --> 00:25:52.460
And we should remain hopeful that there are
prospects for democratization, for opening

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:54.119
to the West.

00:25:54.119 --> 00:25:57.249
But there's several critical timelines at
work here.

00:25:57.249 --> 00:26:04.409
The strictures of the JCPOA become less tight
in seven to 10 years.

00:26:04.409 --> 00:26:09.869
That is exactly the timeline on which I am
most concerned that American leadership will

00:26:09.869 --> 00:26:14.780
drift, that our focus, the priority attention
to this will begin to wander off.

00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:19.039
And that's the moment at which there will
be a greater temptation on the part of the

00:26:19.039 --> 00:26:25.139
Iranian regime if it is, at that point, undergoing
some challenge or some transition domestically

00:26:25.139 --> 00:26:30.749
to return to a nuclear program as a way of
strengthening its nationalist credentials

00:26:30.749 --> 00:26:33.820
and strengthening its regional security profile.

00:26:33.820 --> 00:26:36.919
That's exactly when I think we will be at
the moment of greatest risk.

00:26:36.919 --> 00:26:41.549
So it's my hope that our actions over the
next five years will convince the Iranian

00:26:41.549 --> 00:26:47.509
regime that a return to a nuclear program
is profoundly foolish, that further integration

00:26:47.509 --> 00:26:51.269
into the regional economy and the global economy
is in their best interest.

00:26:51.269 --> 00:26:54.440
And they can only accomplish that by moderating
their behavior.

00:26:54.440 --> 00:27:02.529
IGNATIUS: So I see the warning, insistence
that they abide by the terms of the agreement.

00:27:02.529 --> 00:27:09.539
But I'm still curious about what you think
the United States should do, if anything,

00:27:09.539 --> 00:27:18.690
to encourage a gradual process of political
and cultural change in Iran over the time

00:27:18.690 --> 00:27:20.220
period of this agreement.

00:27:20.220 --> 00:27:27.080
COONS: I think we should be open to and actively
encouraging progress in terms of cultural

00:27:27.080 --> 00:27:32.860
exchanges and engagement with the outside
world, and remain hopeful about the possibility

00:27:32.860 --> 00:27:34.440
of political change.

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:39.190
But that's got to rest on a bedrock of distrust
and a clear-eyed assessment of their current

00:27:39.190 --> 00:27:40.190
actions.

00:27:40.190 --> 00:27:45.619
Their support for the Houthis, the Hezbollah,
for Hamas, their actions in Iraq and Syria

00:27:45.619 --> 00:27:51.759
strongly suggest a country that is not ready
to rejoin the international community.

00:27:51.759 --> 00:27:55.480
They've got problems in accessing the world
economy, in part, because they arbitrarily

00:27:55.480 --> 00:28:00.860
arrest and detain Iranian-American businessmen
who have gone to Iran to try and seek economic

00:28:00.860 --> 00:28:02.349
opportunities.

00:28:02.349 --> 00:28:07.210
They are ratcheting up the number of youth
that they're executing, the number of journalists

00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:08.450
that they're imprisoning.

00:28:08.450 --> 00:28:12.049
Their human rights-recent human rights behavior
is atrocious.

00:28:12.049 --> 00:28:16.820
Now, to launch ballistic missiles that at
least notionally say on the side of them,

00:28:16.820 --> 00:28:22.710
you know, death to Israel, and then express
surprise that the Western world doesn't think

00:28:22.710 --> 00:28:26.769
you're really ready to be part of the global
community just belies credibility.

00:28:26.769 --> 00:28:32.659
So I think we should remain clear-eyed and
active and engaged in pushing back on their

00:28:32.659 --> 00:28:33.659
bad behavior.

00:28:33.659 --> 00:28:39.119
And I think that's the best path, frankly,
towards keeping open the hope or the possibility

00:28:39.119 --> 00:28:40.830
of some reconciliation regionally.

00:28:40.830 --> 00:28:46.169
IGNATIUS: Let me ask a final question from
up here, and then we're going to turn to the

00:28:46.169 --> 00:28:47.169
audience.

00:28:47.169 --> 00:28:50.799
So please be thinking of your questions for
Senator Coons.

00:28:50.799 --> 00:28:59.299
I'd like to ask you to think with us about
Iran's regional behavior.

00:28:59.299 --> 00:29:06.940
It was often said by commentators that the
issue wasn't so much the agreement, which

00:29:06.940 --> 00:29:13.669
was pretty solid, but Iran's bad behavior,
Iran's destabilizing behavior in the region,

00:29:13.669 --> 00:29:15.759
which the nuclear agreement wouldn't address.

00:29:15.759 --> 00:29:26.029
As an observer, I would make the following
contrarian observation, that today compared

00:29:26.029 --> 00:29:33.399
to a year ago Iran seems, to me, to be a little
bit on its back foot in the region.

00:29:33.399 --> 00:29:39.479
The Yemen War, which looked to everyone like
a disaster from the standpoint of the Saudis

00:29:39.479 --> 00:29:45.950
and others, seems to be now meandering towards
a diplomatic resolution.

00:29:45.950 --> 00:29:51.289
There are talks going on in Kuwait between
Houthis and others.

00:29:51.289 --> 00:30:00.320
And there are other quiet talks that are taking
place elsewhere, sponsored, I gather, by the

00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:06.340
United States that are also showing some real
prospects of success.

00:30:06.340 --> 00:30:14.389
A senior Saudi said to me recently: Iran has
turned out to be a paper tiger in Yemen.

00:30:14.389 --> 00:30:15.739
That's one.

00:30:15.739 --> 00:30:18.580
Second, Iran's having a lot of trouble in
Iraq.

00:30:18.580 --> 00:30:22.659
I mean, Iran has having as much trouble being
a hegemon as we did.

00:30:22.659 --> 00:30:26.640
The Shia coalition in Iraq is just fractured.

00:30:26.640 --> 00:30:29.809
The Dawa Party's split in two.

00:30:29.809 --> 00:30:33.730
And Iran doesn't quite seem to know what to
do about it.

00:30:33.730 --> 00:30:42.809
Syria, Iran continues to be trying to help
Bashar al-Assad gain ground, but from what

00:30:42.809 --> 00:30:48.109
we read the Iranians are pretty nervous about
taking casualties in that fight.

00:30:48.109 --> 00:30:50.020
They don't like it.

00:30:50.020 --> 00:30:59.740
And the ayatollah has, from what we hear,
on several occasions tried to reduce his exposure,

00:30:59.740 --> 00:31:04.169
make sure there are fewer coffins coming back
to Tehran, not more.

00:31:04.169 --> 00:31:10.750
So taking those three areas, and I do mean
to ask this as a contrarian question, is it

00:31:10.750 --> 00:31:17.529
possible that Iran is, in fact, losing a little
bit of ground in this regional battlefield?

00:31:17.529 --> 00:31:18.909
And if so, why?

00:31:18.909 --> 00:31:19.909
Why is that happening?

00:31:19.909 --> 00:31:23.840
COONS: Well, I think you put them in the right
order.

00:31:23.840 --> 00:31:29.009
They have failed to be more successful at
projecting force and engagement in Yemen.

00:31:29.009 --> 00:31:33.009
They have failed to be as effective a troublemaker
as they intended to be with the Houthis in

00:31:33.009 --> 00:31:38.450
Yemen, in large part because of the distraction,
the expense, the cost in lives and money of

00:31:38.450 --> 00:31:40.409
their venture in Syria.

00:31:40.409 --> 00:31:46.669
And they've chosen to prioritize bolstering
Assad by shouldering a great deal of the fighting

00:31:46.669 --> 00:31:47.669
burden.

00:31:47.669 --> 00:31:52.239
Assad was in a very different place in the
battlefield a year, year and a half ago.

00:31:52.239 --> 00:31:58.999
And IRGC troops, Shia militias, Shia fighters
from the whole region, from as far as Afghanistan,

00:31:58.999 --> 00:32:04.729
who had been equipped, trained, and deployed
into the Syrian conflict by the Iranians have

00:32:04.729 --> 00:32:12.519
allowed-obviously with active Russian military
help-has allowed Assad to really push back,

00:32:12.519 --> 00:32:15.960
regain a fair amount of ground, and end up
in a quite different place, where today Assad

00:32:15.960 --> 00:32:21.859
can credibly be talking about retaking significant
swaths of Syria.

00:32:21.859 --> 00:32:27.039
So I think their failure to play a decisive
role in Yemen is really because of their distraction

00:32:27.039 --> 00:32:29.399
in both Iraq and Syria.

00:32:29.399 --> 00:32:33.179
To your point, they're discovering that Iraq
is every bit as difficult and demanding a

00:32:33.179 --> 00:32:36.399
swamp as we found it to be.

00:32:36.399 --> 00:32:41.299
I wish for them all the joys of entanglement
and the cost that that can bring.

00:32:41.299 --> 00:32:46.080
But it doesn't show any change in their basic
orientation.

00:32:46.080 --> 00:32:51.059
They've got a significant body of research
scientists that they're not working on developing

00:32:51.059 --> 00:32:54.719
a nuclear weapon, so they're working on developing
ballistic missiles.

00:32:54.719 --> 00:32:57.259
I'm not happy about either of these projects.

00:32:57.259 --> 00:33:02.729
And they have a dramatic shortfall in terms
of what they need to deliver in economic support

00:33:02.729 --> 00:33:08.509
for Rouhani's administration to move forward,
and for them to do anything positive domestically.

00:33:08.509 --> 00:33:13.129
They're pouring a fair amount of the money
they're getting into fixing things domestically,

00:33:13.129 --> 00:33:19.110
but the trajectory of their ballistic missile
program shows, I think, a continued hostile

00:33:19.110 --> 00:33:26.700
intent and a continued desire to play bigger
role as a security threat to the region.

00:33:26.700 --> 00:33:30.950
And I just view this was a temporary challenge,
because of the price of oil, because of the

00:33:30.950 --> 00:33:35.119
difficulty accessing global markets, because
they haven't had the trajectory they hoped

00:33:35.119 --> 00:33:37.139
for economically.

00:33:37.139 --> 00:33:42.619
But they are making clear signs-they are demonstrating
that they have no less intent of spreading

00:33:42.619 --> 00:33:47.730
a revolutionary message in the region and
of continuing to be a real hostile, difficult

00:33:47.730 --> 00:33:48.730
force in the region.

00:33:48.730 --> 00:33:55.889
IGNATIUS: Just to follow up on that, and then
go to the-to the audience.

00:33:55.889 --> 00:34:05.600
It was a common line in criticizing the agreement
that freeing up these Iranian assets that

00:34:05.600 --> 00:34:12.010
were frozen and allowing revenue to come back
into Iran would simply allow Iran to fund

00:34:12.010 --> 00:34:14.440
its adventurism in the region more.

00:34:14.440 --> 00:34:19.460
It was sort of like, well, the IRGC will get
more money to destabilize the region.

00:34:19.460 --> 00:34:23.630
Do you see any-from what you just said about
where the money is going, it doesn't sound

00:34:23.630 --> 00:34:24.659
like that's, in fact, happening.

00:34:24.659 --> 00:34:25.990
But I wanted to ask you that directly.

00:34:25.990 --> 00:34:27.890
COONS: A couple things.

00:34:27.890 --> 00:34:32.740
Several of the presidential candidates in
the other party have repeatedly misstated

00:34:32.740 --> 00:34:34.340
a basic fact.

00:34:34.340 --> 00:34:37.000
Those frozen assets were not U.S. taxpayer
dollars.

00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:41.030
And I found that profoundly irritating, the
frequency with which folks who know better,

00:34:41.030 --> 00:34:45.790
who have been in the briefings, would say
billions of U.S. dollars are going to go to

00:34:45.790 --> 00:34:46.790
Iran.

00:34:46.790 --> 00:34:47.790
That was never the case.

00:34:47.790 --> 00:34:51.750
These are billions of dollars that were frozen
in accounts around the world, in part through

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:54.150
American leadership.

00:34:54.150 --> 00:34:59.590
There were significant disagreements about
how much the Iranians might ever get access

00:34:59.590 --> 00:35:03.870
to, and how quickly, and from what holdings.

00:35:03.870 --> 00:35:06.090
They have received far less than they had
hoped.

00:35:06.090 --> 00:35:10.110
It is taking much longer than they had expected.

00:35:10.110 --> 00:35:13.370
Secretary Lew and others have addressed that
dynamic in public remarks.

00:35:13.370 --> 00:35:16.660
I'm going to be cautious in how much more
I say.

00:35:16.660 --> 00:35:20.970
But I do think that has been an unexpected
dynamic for the Iranians.

00:35:20.970 --> 00:35:25.830
It has been much harder for them to access
their frozen assets around the world than

00:35:25.830 --> 00:35:27.250
they had expected.

00:35:27.250 --> 00:35:30.050
But terrorism is cheap.

00:35:30.050 --> 00:35:37.070
Frankly, the difference between whether they
got $1, $3, $10 or $50 billion is mostly going

00:35:37.070 --> 00:35:41.070
to determine how much they have available
to invest in restoring the infrastructure

00:35:41.070 --> 00:35:43.140
and the economy within Iran.

00:35:43.140 --> 00:35:47.571
The terrorist actions that they have historically
supported are not that expensive, and they

00:35:47.571 --> 00:35:48.940
continue to support them.

00:35:48.940 --> 00:35:55.140
Obviously, they've continued to prioritize
funding their war efforts in Yemen, in Syria,

00:35:55.140 --> 00:35:59.880
in Iraq, over restoring basic services to
their people.

00:35:59.880 --> 00:36:04.260
What I am very pleasantly surprised with is
they haven't significantly increased that.

00:36:04.260 --> 00:36:09.240
I think that's partly because of American
efforts to interdict weapon shipments, to

00:36:09.240 --> 00:36:14.040
sanction entities, to keep our allies engaged
in pushing back.

00:36:14.040 --> 00:36:20.050
And it's partly a choice by the Iranian regime
to pursue economic improvements domestically

00:36:20.050 --> 00:36:25.820
and to focus on the conflict in Syria and
Iraq at the level that they can sustain, rather

00:36:25.820 --> 00:36:26.820
than making it a higher priority.

00:36:26.820 --> 00:36:27.820
IGNATIUS: That's helpful.

00:36:27.820 --> 00:36:32.510
This image of a windfall for terrorism is
not supported by the facts.

00:36:32.510 --> 00:36:33.980
COONS: Not so far.

00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:34.980
IGNATIUS: OK.

00:36:34.980 --> 00:36:35.980
Yes, please.

00:36:35.980 --> 00:36:38.580
And please identify yourselves and, yes.

00:36:38.580 --> 00:36:41.110
Q: Thanks, David.

00:36:41.110 --> 00:36:43.640
I'm Barbara Slavin.

00:36:43.640 --> 00:36:46.610
I run a program on Iran at the Atlantic Council.

00:36:46.610 --> 00:36:49.490
Senator, I wanted to ask a couple questions.

00:36:49.490 --> 00:36:55.530
Do you see any responsibility on the part
of the Saudis for the increase in fighting

00:36:55.530 --> 00:36:57.200
that's been going on in the region?

00:36:57.200 --> 00:37:01.050
You spoke of Iran as trying to export its
revolution, but in fact it's trying to shore

00:37:01.050 --> 00:37:04.410
up a dictatorial regime in Syria.

00:37:04.410 --> 00:37:07.720
That's not very revolutionary, in my view.

00:37:07.720 --> 00:37:09.290
What do you think of the Boeing deal?

00:37:09.290 --> 00:37:10.340
Is that good?

00:37:10.340 --> 00:37:11.950
Is that something we should encourage?

00:37:11.950 --> 00:37:13.910
Should Congress try to block it?

00:37:13.910 --> 00:37:20.160
And finally, how do we institutionalize the
contacts that we have with the Iranians through

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:21.740
the JCPOA?

00:37:21.740 --> 00:37:25.680
Should there be an effort to try to send Americans
to staff an intersection?

00:37:25.680 --> 00:37:30.520
Should there be some way of doing more than
having occasional meetings in Vienna?

00:37:30.520 --> 00:37:31.520
Thanks.

00:37:31.520 --> 00:37:33.500
COONS: Those are three great questions.

00:37:33.500 --> 00:37:36.060
Let me work through them backwards, if I might.

00:37:36.060 --> 00:37:43.320
The importance of the personal relationships
that both Secretary Moniz and Secretary Kerry

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:51.450
enjoyed, you know, with Rouhani and Zarif
and Salehi, have been central, I think, to

00:37:51.450 --> 00:37:55.100
the effective implementation of the deal so
far.

00:37:55.100 --> 00:38:01.500
If Secretary Moniz is made aware of a technical
issue that pushes the boundaries or the definitions

00:38:01.500 --> 00:38:05.970
or challenges some of the operation of this
deal, he's able to pick up the phone and directly

00:38:05.970 --> 00:38:11.090
speak to someone he's known for decades, and
where they are both highly technically competent

00:38:11.090 --> 00:38:12.090
themselves.

00:38:12.090 --> 00:38:18.960
They are not relying on, you know, some series
of staffers to get them up to speed on exactly

00:38:18.960 --> 00:38:22.900
how a centrifuge works and exactly what a
rotary means, and so forth.

00:38:22.900 --> 00:38:24.850
We have to sustain that.

00:38:24.850 --> 00:38:29.740
We have to invest, to your point about how
important are the institutional relationships.

00:38:29.740 --> 00:38:33.040
Whoever the next president is, whoever the
next secretary of state and secretary of energy

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:38.900
is, assuming that they have the wisdom to
try and sustain the JCPOA, I think they have

00:38:38.900 --> 00:38:43.820
to invest in those direct relationships and
those direct channels communication.

00:38:43.820 --> 00:38:45.910
And I think that is going to be critical.

00:38:45.910 --> 00:38:51.410
I am pleased that Ambassador Mull has a competent
multiagency team that is deeply experienced

00:38:51.410 --> 00:38:53.240
and is staying on this.

00:38:53.240 --> 00:38:54.850
And I think we have to sustain that as well.

00:38:54.850 --> 00:39:01.190
I also think it's important to sustain congressional
familiarity with the technical details and

00:39:01.190 --> 00:39:04.590
engagement, a better, broader understanding
of the region.

00:39:04.590 --> 00:39:11.150
Look, I've only been to Saudi Arabia once
myself, to Turkey once, I've never been to

00:39:11.150 --> 00:39:12.150
Iran-surprise.

00:39:12.150 --> 00:39:16.720
I do think that making sure that members of
Congress, both houses, both parties, know

00:39:16.720 --> 00:39:20.730
the context, know the region, are engaged,
is also going to be critically important.

00:39:20.730 --> 00:39:22.380
I don't know what to say about the Boeing
deal.

00:39:22.380 --> 00:39:25.220
Their argument is, where Airbus goes, we go.

00:39:25.220 --> 00:39:29.500
Why would we deny opportunity to American
workers and machinists to be able to manufacture

00:39:29.500 --> 00:39:32.540
and sell world-class airline-air frames?

00:39:32.540 --> 00:39:37.870
And it is specifically called out in the JCPOA
as a specific market segment that we're to

00:39:37.870 --> 00:39:39.440
try and participate in.

00:39:39.440 --> 00:39:45.200
I leave it to Boeing to assess the risk to
them, whether or not the Iranians really are

00:39:45.200 --> 00:39:50.100
credit worthy, whether or not this is an economic
engagement they really want to get more closely

00:39:50.100 --> 00:39:51.100
involved in.

00:39:51.100 --> 00:39:57.840
But it is a sign of American economic interest
moving up a step.

00:39:57.840 --> 00:40:02.780
And I think it is a sign to the Iranians that
it is potentially possible to do business

00:40:02.780 --> 00:40:06.360
with one of America's premier manufacturing
companies.

00:40:06.360 --> 00:40:11.090
It's also a reminder that countries around
the world, even rogue states like Iran, prefer

00:40:11.090 --> 00:40:16.520
American products and services, which I'll
just say with a small boosterism smile on

00:40:16.520 --> 00:40:17.520
my face.

00:40:17.520 --> 00:40:19.470
But I do think it-
IGNATIUS: What about Barbara's question about

00:40:19.470 --> 00:40:22.590
a U.S. intersection?

00:40:22.590 --> 00:40:27.560
COONS: My hope is that that's something we
could move towards.

00:40:27.560 --> 00:40:32.710
Their behavior is among the worst in the world
when it comes to their treatment of-number

00:40:32.710 --> 00:40:40.260
of executions, and treatment of journalists,
detention of Americans, and failure to account

00:40:40.260 --> 00:40:43.560
for Americans who we know have disappeared
into their custody.

00:40:43.560 --> 00:40:50.220
Look, we've recently reopened relations with
Cuba, in a robust way, for the first time

00:40:50.220 --> 00:40:51.560
in decades.

00:40:51.560 --> 00:40:55.920
If we can move in a positive direction that
sustains those open channels of communication,

00:40:55.920 --> 00:40:57.770
I think that's well worth doing.

00:40:57.770 --> 00:40:58.920
Q: (Off mic.)

00:40:58.920 --> 00:41:06.350
COONS: You know, that's a complex relationship
that's become more urgent, I think, on both

00:41:06.350 --> 00:41:07.740
sides.

00:41:07.740 --> 00:41:09.790
There is new leadership in the kingdom.

00:41:09.790 --> 00:41:16.510
And I'm hoping that there will be a real reexamination
of our long and close alliance.

00:41:16.510 --> 00:41:23.070
I think the challenges-the security challenges
that they faced in Yemen has renewed a focus

00:41:23.070 --> 00:41:28.520
on the competency and the engagement of their
military at all levels.

00:41:28.520 --> 00:41:33.340
I think they've seen the United States come
to their support in a prompt and thorough

00:41:33.340 --> 00:41:35.810
and meaningful way in the conflict in Yemen.

00:41:35.810 --> 00:41:39.480
I think that has reassured them that we are
their allies, and we intend to work closely

00:41:39.480 --> 00:41:41.290
with them and stand by them.

00:41:41.290 --> 00:41:48.460
But there is an enduring question about their
role and their relationship with elements

00:41:48.460 --> 00:41:55.380
within the Muslim world that have preached
a version of Islam that may have led, in some

00:41:55.380 --> 00:42:00.880
parts of the world, to some more extreme views
and some more aggressive actions.

00:42:00.880 --> 00:42:04.720
That's a delicate conversation and a difficult
one to have, but an important one to continue

00:42:04.720 --> 00:42:06.540
having between friends.

00:42:06.540 --> 00:42:08.950
IGNATIUS: The gentleman here.

00:42:08.950 --> 00:42:10.150
Yes, please.

00:42:10.150 --> 00:42:17.840
Q: As you well know, most of the region's
critics of the agreement are indifferent to

00:42:17.840 --> 00:42:23.170
its details and to even the efficacy of the
agreement.

00:42:23.170 --> 00:42:29.210
They put it in the context of a United States
that providentially shows up.

00:42:29.210 --> 00:42:34.550
When Iran is in trouble with Saddam, the Americans
end up destroying Saddam.

00:42:34.550 --> 00:42:40.130
When the Taliban are on the verge of war with
Iran, the Americans come in and destroy the

00:42:40.130 --> 00:42:41.130
narrative.

00:42:41.130 --> 00:42:42.130
There is this narrative.

00:42:42.130 --> 00:42:46.820
Of course, it construes much more to these
events than they really carry.

00:42:46.820 --> 00:42:53.730
But in that context, I believe that the evaluation
of the agreement is much influenced by our

00:42:53.730 --> 00:43:00.270
current behavior in responding to the Iranian
provocation that you presented.

00:43:00.270 --> 00:43:06.450
Even the little boat episode, which is here
forgotten-the boat episode was played up hugely

00:43:06.450 --> 00:43:07.870
on the media.

00:43:07.870 --> 00:43:15.390
There, they showed the American woman sailor,
officer, being forced to wear, they repeated

00:43:15.390 --> 00:43:17.330
these scenes of humiliation.

00:43:17.330 --> 00:43:19.410
And no American response.

00:43:19.410 --> 00:43:21.970
The Americans don't respond.

00:43:21.970 --> 00:43:28.190
Then there is, of course, on the ballistic
missiles, and you-well, again, you very carefully

00:43:28.190 --> 00:43:33.650
observed there was no American response to
that, even though it appeared to be a very

00:43:33.650 --> 00:43:40.220
deliberate provocation because of the proximity
in date to the signature to the launch and

00:43:40.220 --> 00:43:41.220
so on.

00:43:41.220 --> 00:43:46.640
Finally, even in countries whose human rights
situation is appalling, they do keep saying

00:43:46.640 --> 00:43:53.650
the Americans make no effort to advance human
rights or to do-the typical American things

00:43:53.650 --> 00:43:58.220
when America wants to interfere in a country's
internal arrangement the Americans don't do

00:43:58.220 --> 00:44:04.480
in Iran, and some people connect it to the
failure to even mention the Green Revolution

00:44:04.480 --> 00:44:06.100
on the part of the White House.

00:44:06.100 --> 00:44:08.290
And as you know, there's a whole polemic with
that.

00:44:08.290 --> 00:44:13.530
So I believe that, going forward, the actual-
IGNATIUS: Let's get this as a question-

00:44:13.530 --> 00:44:15.020
Q: That is the question.

00:44:15.020 --> 00:44:20.050
Do you believe that, going forward, it is
possible to uphold, as it were, the validity

00:44:20.050 --> 00:44:27.400
of the agreement by changing the tone of U.S.
responses to Iran, or whether that would cause

00:44:27.400 --> 00:44:30.160
the unraveling of the agreement?

00:44:30.160 --> 00:44:31.340
Because you-
COONS: Yes.

00:44:31.340 --> 00:44:33.100
Q: That is the answer?

00:44:33.100 --> 00:44:34.680
That you-
COONS: I'm sorry.

00:44:34.680 --> 00:44:43.070
I do think-you pointed to a very wide range
of conspiracy theories, incidents of provocation-let

00:44:43.070 --> 00:44:44.980
me try to answer.

00:44:44.980 --> 00:44:50.310
You've pointed to a whole series of conspiracy
theories, incidents that happened without

00:44:50.310 --> 00:44:54.350
aggressive response from the United States,
and then other episodes that have gotten a

00:44:54.350 --> 00:44:57.720
response from the United States, and then
some of the ongoing challenges, the tension

00:44:57.720 --> 00:45:03.060
between our putting human rights at the top
of our agenda or putting security at the top

00:45:03.060 --> 00:45:04.060
of our agenda.

00:45:04.060 --> 00:45:06.080
That's a very broad range of topics.

00:45:06.080 --> 00:45:12.550
Briefly put, I do think we have to be measured
in our response to provocations by Iran, but

00:45:12.550 --> 00:45:17.270
I think we have to be firm and prompt and
act in concert with our allies.

00:45:17.270 --> 00:45:23.110
I may disagree or quibble over specific incidents
and how the administration responded and why.

00:45:23.110 --> 00:45:27.010
I may not have full information about what
the factors were they were weighing at the

00:45:27.010 --> 00:45:28.010
time.

00:45:28.010 --> 00:45:33.830
All I know is that going forward I think the
next administration has to place the effective

00:45:33.830 --> 00:45:39.150
implementation and enforcement of this deal
high on their list of priorities at a time

00:45:39.150 --> 00:45:41.190
when it's going to be hard to do that.

00:45:41.190 --> 00:45:45.700
There's lots of other distractions in the
world, whether it's North Korea or Russia,

00:45:45.700 --> 00:45:49.510
whether it's the South China Sea or the status
of NATO, there's a lot of other things we

00:45:49.510 --> 00:45:51.590
can and should be worrying about.

00:45:51.590 --> 00:45:58.030
But we can't ignore the ways in which this
deal and our relationship with Iran influence

00:45:58.030 --> 00:46:03.190
and centrally effect the outcome in Syria
and Iraq, influence and centrally affect our

00:46:03.190 --> 00:46:07.910
relationship with Russia, influence and centrally
affect the strength and importance of NATO

00:46:07.910 --> 00:46:08.990
and the EU.

00:46:08.990 --> 00:46:10.620
These are all tied together.

00:46:10.620 --> 00:46:16.630
My concern, centrally-the point of my remarks
today-is a concern that this will drop from

00:46:16.630 --> 00:46:21.070
view, as it largely has in the debates of
this presidential campaign.

00:46:21.070 --> 00:46:27.030
My view is that it has to be higher in priority
and we have to be consistent in how we respond,

00:46:27.030 --> 00:46:30.330
whether to provocations or to developments
in the region.

00:46:30.330 --> 00:46:34.740
IGNATIUS: So I'm going to recognize people
in the order in which I've seen them.

00:46:34.740 --> 00:46:41.690
The woman here at the front table, and then
you, sir.

00:46:41.690 --> 00:46:44.210
Q: Louise Shelley, George Mason University.

00:46:44.210 --> 00:46:47.310
You haven't talked about Turkey.

00:46:47.310 --> 00:46:52.720
And I just came back from weeks in Turkey
where everybody is talking about the case

00:46:52.720 --> 00:47:00.850
of Reza, who helped move millions or billions
of Iranian money through Turkey.

00:47:00.850 --> 00:47:04.821
And Turks are still concerned, either positively
or negatively, that either that they should

00:47:04.821 --> 00:47:08.910
be helping their neighbor Iran in the time
of economic difficulties and provide them

00:47:08.910 --> 00:47:15.110
access to the financial system, or rather
that this is fostering corruption in Turkey.

00:47:15.110 --> 00:47:20.380
And how do you perceive this issue of Turkey
being-you know, having facilitated so much

00:47:20.380 --> 00:47:25.660
of Iran's money movements, as Turkey is a
NATO member and an ally of ours?

00:47:25.660 --> 00:47:33.190
COONS: Well, our relationship with Turkey
is complex, difficult, and also an urgent

00:47:33.190 --> 00:47:34.190
priority.

00:47:34.190 --> 00:47:40.610
Our differences over how to respond to ISIS
and Assad, our differences in how we view

00:47:40.610 --> 00:47:46.020
the Peshmerga, the Kurdish question have been
a source of real tension and challenge.

00:47:46.020 --> 00:47:53.750
And how they're responding to security challenges,
how they're continuing to develop their democracy,

00:47:53.750 --> 00:47:58.960
and some of the challenges within the governing
party, I think are all sources of legitimate

00:47:58.960 --> 00:48:01.310
concern in the United States.

00:48:01.310 --> 00:48:07.330
We need and want a closer, stronger, more
reliable partnership with Turkey.

00:48:07.330 --> 00:48:11.790
I don't know enough about the details of how
money was moved, how much money was moved,

00:48:11.790 --> 00:48:17.700
and what the corruption consequences might
be or the security considerations might be.

00:48:17.700 --> 00:48:22.600
But we're going to face comparable challenges
with countries all around Iran, as the Iranians

00:48:22.600 --> 00:48:26.940
seek more and more avenues to access the global
markets.

00:48:26.940 --> 00:48:32.640
There are billions of dollars that are or
will be moving that have been frozen in banks

00:48:32.640 --> 00:48:36.240
or that are waiting to reach the global markets.

00:48:36.240 --> 00:48:39.210
And we still have sanctions on the books.

00:48:39.210 --> 00:48:41.420
And we are going to continue to enforce those
sanctions.

00:48:41.420 --> 00:48:46.890
And that means there will be unexpected and
perhaps unwelcome pathways of leakage in and

00:48:46.890 --> 00:48:48.320
out of the Iranian economy.

00:48:48.320 --> 00:48:53.080
So I simply point to this-thank you for raising
it-and I'll point to it as just another source

00:48:53.080 --> 00:48:58.190
of playing in the joints or strain on our
vital regional alliances.

00:48:58.190 --> 00:49:02.590
My hope is that we don't overemphasize it
in a relationship with Turkey where we have

00:49:02.590 --> 00:49:08.810
so many other issues going on that are short
term, pressing, and tactically vital.

00:49:08.810 --> 00:49:12.510
IGNATIUS: Mr. Cunningham.

00:49:12.510 --> 00:49:14.000
Q: Thank you.

00:49:14.000 --> 00:49:15.830
Nelson Cunningham, McLarty Associates.

00:49:15.830 --> 00:49:19.710
First of all, I'm sure everyone comes away
from this wishing that every actor in our

00:49:19.710 --> 00:49:25.010
political space brought as much intellect
and, frankly, painstaking care as you bring

00:49:25.010 --> 00:49:27.950
to an important issue like this one.

00:49:27.950 --> 00:49:33.820
In the course of the last several months I
spent an hour and a half with President Rouhani,

00:49:33.820 --> 00:49:36.240
along with a group of American business people.

00:49:36.240 --> 00:49:43.030
And in February, a similar amount of time
with Foreign Minister Zarif.

00:49:43.030 --> 00:49:50.610
You've made it clear that from your perspective
this is a transactional-a transaction not

00:49:50.610 --> 00:49:52.240
a transformation.

00:49:52.240 --> 00:49:56.900
I took from their comments that they believe
this is potentially transformational.

00:49:56.900 --> 00:50:02.890
And in fact, Rouhani and Zarif both made fairly
impassioned pleas to the members of the European

00:50:02.890 --> 00:50:06.791
and the American business community to help
give them the economic benefits that they

00:50:06.791 --> 00:50:12.990
believe they had promised the Iranian people
so that they could achieve their wish of making

00:50:12.990 --> 00:50:14.720
this transformational.

00:50:14.720 --> 00:50:18.150
But it's also plain that within the Iranian
government there are those who have different

00:50:18.150 --> 00:50:19.150
views.

00:50:19.150 --> 00:50:21.820
Rouhani and Zarif do not control the entire
government.

00:50:21.820 --> 00:50:27.120
The Revolutionary Guard, as you pointed out,
takes a very different perspective.

00:50:27.120 --> 00:50:34.120
To re-ask a question that David Ignatius asked
before, isn't there the possibility that by

00:50:34.120 --> 00:50:39.930
helping to bring more commercial engagement
from the U.S. we could, indeed, be helping

00:50:39.930 --> 00:50:45.690
an important internal conversation in Iran
between those like Rouhani and Zarif, who

00:50:45.690 --> 00:50:50.560
perhaps share our values more, against those
within the government who do not?

00:50:50.560 --> 00:50:53.830
COONS: I think there is that possibility,
yes.

00:50:53.830 --> 00:50:59.350
And I think, as I tried to say before but
perhaps was unartful in how I conveyed it,

00:50:59.350 --> 00:51:05.410
I think we have to remain hopeful, optimistic,
open to the possibility of the Iranian regime

00:51:05.410 --> 00:51:10.980
reforming and changing, and to the possibility
that economic liberalization and opening to

00:51:10.980 --> 00:51:13.580
the West will lead to that.

00:51:13.580 --> 00:51:15.460
We are an optimistic people.

00:51:15.460 --> 00:51:22.130
And we have to conduct foreign policy with
a priority on hope, informed by a clear-eyed,

00:51:22.130 --> 00:51:30.730
skeptical, deep distrust of a regime whose
current actions demonstrate not just a disinterest

00:51:30.730 --> 00:51:34.340
in rejoining the world community, but an active
disregard for it.

00:51:34.340 --> 00:51:37.110
Now, obviously, it's not a monolithic regime.

00:51:37.110 --> 00:51:38.340
There are internal tensions.

00:51:38.340 --> 00:51:42.110
There are elements within the regime that
may be carrying out things like capturing

00:51:42.110 --> 00:51:48.110
American sailors, or launching ballistic missiles,
or refusing to share internally what they

00:51:48.110 --> 00:51:52.370
know about an American who's been missing
for years that's in their custody.

00:51:52.370 --> 00:51:56.890
I recognize that, you know, there is no more
a unitary monolithic government within Iran

00:51:56.890 --> 00:51:58.900
than there is within the United States.

00:51:58.900 --> 00:52:03.350
But we have to be clear-eyed about their current
behavior, their past behavior, and its likely

00:52:03.350 --> 00:52:04.750
trajectory.

00:52:04.750 --> 00:52:09.270
I do think that-many of my colleagues have
met with Rouhani and Zarif and come back and

00:52:09.270 --> 00:52:13.700
reported glowing things about their intentions
and their values and their priorities.

00:52:13.700 --> 00:52:16.240
And all of that is good.

00:52:16.240 --> 00:52:23.000
But I'm simply trying to push back on an optimism
that is not yet justified by actions.

00:52:23.000 --> 00:52:28.260
In terms of the transaction of freezing their
progress towards enough fissile material for

00:52:28.260 --> 00:52:32.530
a weapon, this agreement has been a success
so far.

00:52:32.530 --> 00:52:37.590
But as an avenue towards transformation, I
think it will only be successful if we continue

00:52:37.590 --> 00:52:41.540
to insist on the transaction being successful
first.

00:52:41.540 --> 00:52:47.730
IGNATIUS: So the gentleman all the way in
the back with-yes-his hand raised.

00:52:47.730 --> 00:52:49.660
Yes, sir.

00:52:49.660 --> 00:52:52.540
Q: I'm Haik Gugarats with Argus Media.

00:52:52.540 --> 00:52:57.250
Senator, is there room for one more transactional
agreement?

00:52:57.250 --> 00:53:04.750
And what could Iran be asked to give up in
such an agreement?

00:53:04.750 --> 00:53:07.890
COONS: Interesting.

00:53:07.890 --> 00:53:12.751
There's a number of things that are much smaller
in scope than the nuclear program, but that

00:53:12.751 --> 00:53:18.740
I think are of real significance in priority.

00:53:18.740 --> 00:53:25.450
A ballistic missile program that is demonstrating
a greater and greater sophistication and capability

00:53:25.450 --> 00:53:31.960
of delivering a nuclear weapon, should they
ever return to a nuclear weapons program.

00:53:31.960 --> 00:53:38.100
Domestic enrichment capacity, which I understand
the dynamic between sovereignty and being

00:53:38.100 --> 00:53:42.210
respected as a country with engineering capabilities
and so forth.

00:53:42.210 --> 00:53:49.900
But I really question the legitimacy of an
expressed need for an industrial-scale nuclear

00:53:49.900 --> 00:53:50.900
enrichment program.

00:53:50.900 --> 00:53:56.870
There has been a fair amount of quibbling
about was this a good deal, a weak deal, a

00:53:56.870 --> 00:53:57.870
terrible deal.

00:53:57.870 --> 00:54:03.650
Those who criticize the deal at its simplest
level say: They're allowed to retain a domestic

00:54:03.650 --> 00:54:04.710
enrichment capability.

00:54:04.710 --> 00:54:06.860
There is no need for that.

00:54:06.860 --> 00:54:11.400
They could continue to have nuclear reactors
to do research, to do nuclear medicine, but

00:54:11.400 --> 00:54:15.710
have no enrichment and processing capability
within the country.

00:54:15.710 --> 00:54:20.330
Some would argue that the IAEA inspections
and the restrictions of the JCPOA largely

00:54:20.330 --> 00:54:23.150
deal with that, but I am concerned.

00:54:23.150 --> 00:54:25.120
I'm concerned about its path forward.

00:54:25.120 --> 00:54:28.230
I'm concerned about its trajectory more than
10 years from now.

00:54:28.230 --> 00:54:33.620
So if there were two things that, to me, would
be the lynchpins of a next-stage transactional

00:54:33.620 --> 00:54:38.940
deal, it would be Iran being willing to give
up on the continued development of ballistic

00:54:38.940 --> 00:54:44.580
missiles capable of delivering nuclear weapons
regionally, and to give up on an ongoing nuclear

00:54:44.580 --> 00:54:46.940
enrichment capability.

00:54:46.940 --> 00:54:52.710
There should be significant further opportunities,
further engagement with the economy and the

00:54:52.710 --> 00:54:54.640
world community were they to do that.

00:54:54.640 --> 00:54:58.640
But that would be the sort of stage two or
JCPOA 2.0.

00:54:58.640 --> 00:55:03.300
IGNATIUS: That's a fascinating answer to a
fascinating question.

00:55:03.300 --> 00:55:10.720
And I want to just ask, would you-let's imagine
that Secretary Clinton is in the White House

00:55:10.720 --> 00:55:12.020
next January.

00:55:12.020 --> 00:55:20.910
Would you urge her to consider a new round
of this engagement with Iran on key strategic

00:55:20.910 --> 00:55:25.010
issues, the two you mentioned, as a follow
on?

00:55:25.010 --> 00:55:26.960
COONS: I would.

00:55:26.960 --> 00:55:32.240
IGNATIUS: Yes, at this second table, if you're
still on.

00:55:32.240 --> 00:55:33.730
Yes.

00:55:33.730 --> 00:55:35.510
Q: Thank you.

00:55:35.510 --> 00:55:37.890
Amy Nelson with the Council on Foreign Relations.

00:55:37.890 --> 00:55:45.070
I wanted to ask you about IAEA support and
capacity, given that 20 percent of its existing

00:55:45.070 --> 00:55:49.750
resources are now devoted to the implementation
of the JCPOA.

00:55:49.750 --> 00:55:54.930
And given the significant need-ongoing need
for safeguards and their implementation and

00:55:54.930 --> 00:55:59.840
verification, it would seem the IAEA might
be on the course to a crisis.

00:55:59.840 --> 00:56:04.470
What can and should the United States and
the international community do to ensure that

00:56:04.470 --> 00:56:06.580
it doesn't reach a crisis point?

00:56:06.580 --> 00:56:11.980
And second, I wanted to ask you about your
comments on disputes in the implementation

00:56:11.980 --> 00:56:13.930
of the JCPOA.

00:56:13.930 --> 00:56:19.360
Given that certain language in the deal was
negotiated to be deliberately vague-for example,

00:56:19.360 --> 00:56:27.770
as safeguard technology evolves we need flexibility
in terms of how its implemented.

00:56:27.770 --> 00:56:32.420
And if you can comment on this, to what extent
were the disputes that we've experienced so

00:56:32.420 --> 00:56:39.330
far a function of either this vague language
or sort of technical obstacles to implementation?

00:56:39.330 --> 00:56:43.460
COONS: Good questions.

00:56:43.460 --> 00:56:50.530
On the second part of your question, I don't
think we've yet reached the point where some

00:56:50.530 --> 00:56:55.950
of the linguistic challenges you're referring
to have yet become operational.

00:56:55.950 --> 00:56:59.950
I think the boundary fights have been much
more simple.

00:56:59.950 --> 00:57:04.980
But willful misunderstandings if fairly basic
points that are relatively easy to say you

00:57:04.980 --> 00:57:10.150
say this, we say this, agreement says this,
do this, so far.

00:57:10.150 --> 00:57:16.610
But I think as time goes on, there may be
more challenges that are closer to the dynamic

00:57:16.610 --> 00:57:22.160
you describe, where there is the opportunity
for litigating boundaries.

00:57:22.160 --> 00:57:29.820
If nothing else, the Iranians are spectacularly
persistent at arguing lots of points.

00:57:29.820 --> 00:57:35.650
My concern is that we remain spectacularly
engaged and determined and persistent around

00:57:35.650 --> 00:57:42.800
procurement channel questions and at elevating
and resolving in favor of not providing access

00:57:42.800 --> 00:57:46.710
to more advanced technology, not providing
more room for a robust program.

00:57:46.710 --> 00:57:52.000
I'm sorry, the first part of your question
was?

00:57:52.000 --> 00:57:53.480
Q: Support for IAEA.

00:57:53.480 --> 00:57:55.080
COONS: Support for IAEA, right.

00:57:55.080 --> 00:58:01.340
Look, we are asking the IAEA to do the biggest,
broadest, toughest job it's ever done.

00:58:01.340 --> 00:58:07.170
And as I pointed out to Amano, and many of
colleagues have, this is an existential moment

00:58:07.170 --> 00:58:08.210
for them.

00:58:08.210 --> 00:58:13.970
This is the task that they will either truly
succeed at, and reinforce their role as the

00:58:13.970 --> 00:58:24.400
world's absolutely indisputable, necessary
atomic watchdog, or where they will miss the

00:58:24.400 --> 00:58:29.100
reemergence and development of a clandestine
program, and the consequences would be significant,

00:58:29.100 --> 00:58:33.010
but for their reputation and for global security.

00:58:33.010 --> 00:58:38.460
I think we need to be demonstrating a long-term
commitment to robust funding.

00:58:38.460 --> 00:58:43.920
And the IAEA being a member agency, an entity
whose budget is made up of contributions of

00:58:43.920 --> 00:58:50.200
many member states, it's concerned about not
being overly funded by the United States,

00:58:50.200 --> 00:58:53.840
overly dependent on American financial and
technical support.

00:58:53.840 --> 00:58:54.840
And I respect that.

00:58:54.840 --> 00:59:00.020
And so I think we need to be finding ways
to ensure that the voluntary contributions

00:59:00.020 --> 00:59:07.700
of all member states rise in accordance with
the complexity and reach of the IAEA's mission,

00:59:07.700 --> 00:59:14.080
which continues to be global in scope, where
Iran is not the only or most difficult country

00:59:14.080 --> 00:59:21.220
it's trying to monitor, but where the deployment
of new cutting-edge, real-time enrichment

00:59:21.220 --> 00:59:27.600
monitoring capabilities of new real-time monitoring
and remote-sensing capabilities really have

00:59:27.600 --> 00:59:31.270
the possibility to show what the IAEA can
do in this century.

00:59:31.270 --> 00:59:36.110
The United States should continue to increase
funding for our domestic research into those

00:59:36.110 --> 00:59:37.330
technologies and capabilities.

00:59:37.330 --> 00:59:43.240
And our national labs should continue to provide
the training and the support that makes cutting-edge

00:59:43.240 --> 00:59:47.600
nuclear inspectors that are well-trained and
properly deployed possible.

00:59:47.600 --> 00:59:52.520
But more than anything, we have to show that
we are a reliable funder, because if you are

00:59:52.520 --> 00:59:58.881
looking at ramping up the number of inspectors
you can deploy by dozens or hundreds, you

00:59:58.881 --> 01:00:05.240
don't just go on Monster.com and hire a bunch
of nuclear inspectors willing to go live,

01:00:05.240 --> 01:00:09.300
you know, in the Middle East on long stints
at the drop of a hat.

01:00:09.300 --> 01:00:14.210
This requires a sustained, long-term investment,
something for which I'm fighting in the Appropriations

01:00:14.210 --> 01:00:18.320
Committee, and that I expect will enjoy bipartisan
support for a long time.

01:00:18.320 --> 01:00:22.700
IGNATIUS: I'm going to recognize one more
hand, that's Ambassador Cutler.

01:00:22.700 --> 01:00:26.540
But I want to note we've come to the end of
our time.

01:00:26.540 --> 01:00:36.910
So, Walt, please, a 30-second question and,
Senator, a 35-second answer.

01:00:36.910 --> 01:00:37.910
(Laughter.)

01:00:37.910 --> 01:00:42.850
Q: Very quickly, and I return to the initial
question that Barbara posed, regarding support

01:00:42.850 --> 01:00:50.290
for our friends, our partners in the Middle
East-here, I refer to the Gulf States particularly-who

01:00:50.290 --> 01:00:58.000
obviously recognize that Iran poses a continuing
security threat, as you put it.

01:00:58.000 --> 01:01:00.130
And we're being very supportive, I think.

01:01:00.130 --> 01:01:05.330
And the Congress, I think, is being terribly
supportive, which is very good.

01:01:05.330 --> 01:01:11.300
Largest military sales, I think, in the history
of our country to the Gulf States.

01:01:11.300 --> 01:01:17.970
But what do you say to those partners of ours
who say, look, we should start planning our

01:01:17.970 --> 01:01:20.710
own nuclear capabilities?

01:01:20.710 --> 01:01:28.690
COONS: That is one of the greatest potential
risks of an unraveling of the JCPOA.

01:01:28.690 --> 01:01:32.380
So thank you for reminding me for failing
to put on the agenda of things that would

01:01:32.380 --> 01:01:39.500
go badly quickly if a next American president
were to walk away from the deal, or in a ham-fisted

01:01:39.500 --> 01:01:42.510
way attempt to renegotiate it and fail.

01:01:42.510 --> 01:01:48.210
The speed with which some of our Gulf allies
would move towards the acquisition of a nuclear

01:01:48.210 --> 01:01:52.420
weapon or a development of a domestic enrichment
program I think would be truly unsettling

01:01:52.420 --> 01:01:57.400
and could lead to a significant increase in
regional tensions and risks.

01:01:57.400 --> 01:02:03.510
So our best move is to continue to reinforce
to regional partners and allies our commitment

01:02:03.510 --> 01:02:07.890
to their security, our determination to provide
them with the conventional defense means they

01:02:07.890 --> 01:02:14.600
need, and to make it clear to them that we
will be clear-eyed, persistent, engaged, enthusiastic

01:02:14.600 --> 01:02:20.220
about enforcement of the JCPOA, and the containment
of any Iranian nuclear ambitions, to forestall

01:02:20.220 --> 01:02:25.390
any belief on their part that they need to
have a fallback position and be prepared to

01:02:25.390 --> 01:02:27.180
develop their own nuclear weapons.

01:02:27.180 --> 01:02:33.190
The development of a nuclear contest between
Pakistan and India was one of the riskiest,

01:02:33.190 --> 01:02:36.000
most destabilizing developments of the last
century.

01:02:36.000 --> 01:02:41.470
The nuclearized Korean Peninsula is showing
us even today the very real threats to security

01:02:41.470 --> 01:02:43.130
posed by North Korea.

01:02:43.130 --> 01:02:47.630
To have a nuclear-armed Iran and then a nuclear-armed
Saudi Arabia, and a nuclear-armed Kuwait,

01:02:47.630 --> 01:02:53.540
and a nuclear-armed Qatar would be disastrous,
and something we should work very hard to

01:02:53.540 --> 01:02:54.540
prevent.

01:02:54.540 --> 01:03:00.730
IGNATIUS: So, with apologies to those I couldn't
call on, I'm going to bring this to a close.

01:03:00.730 --> 01:03:04.490
We'll invite Senator Coons to come back a
year from now, and we can look at two years

01:03:04.490 --> 01:03:05.490
on.

01:03:05.490 --> 01:03:06.490
(Laughter.)

01:03:06.490 --> 01:03:11.200
Senator, just to echo what Nelson Cunningham
said, it's really a pleasure to have someone

01:03:11.200 --> 01:03:15.100
join us here at the Council who is so knowledgeable
about his subject.

01:03:15.100 --> 01:03:16.330
We've all learned a lot.

01:03:16.330 --> 01:03:17.570
So thank you very much.

01:03:17.570 --> 01:03:18.570
(Applause.)

01:03:18.570 --> 01:03:18.571
(END)

